ISO -- the isolation statistic:  ISO(k) = delta[run 11 (5 epochs at keep=1.00)](k) - delta[run 9  (trained at keep=0.50)](k)
==============================================================================
  delta_c(k) = recall_c(k) - recall_c(1.00), paired per image, ink tokens.
  Negative ISO = the focus checkpoint is hurt MORE by identical pruning.
     keep  d_focus    d_ref      ISO    SE      t  resolution   rho  power
     0.50    -1.45    +0.60    -2.05  2.22  -0.92       4.44 pts -0.06  UNDERPOWERED
     0.35    -8.40    -0.26    -8.15  2.71  -3.01 *     5.42 pts -0.02  UNDERPOWERED
     0.25   -14.31    -6.96    -7.35  2.72  -2.70 *     5.45 pts  0.48  UNDERPOWERED
     0.20   -20.93   -13.01    -7.92  2.48  -3.19 *     4.97 pts  0.56  UNDERPOWERED
  rho = per-image correlation of the two delta columns. It, not the means, sets the
  resolution: on D12's run-5/run-9 pair rho was 0.14-0.57 and all four rows were
  UNDERPOWERED. n cannot be raised; 50 IS the FUNSD test split.
  identity check: max |paired ISO - (d_focus - d_ref)| = 1.78e-15  (asserted <= 1e-09)

  => H1 ISOLATED: H1 ISOLATED. ISO is -8.15 pts at keep=0.35 (SE 2.71, t -3.01) and <= 0 at every budget, so identical pruning costs the FOCUS checkpoint more than the REFERENCE one with the epoch count held constant. Pruning-aware training causes the robustness; Claim 1 may say 'because it was trained for it'.

